Nepalese authorities will launch a risky effort this fiscal year to drain four high-mountain glacial lakes in a bid to mitigate flood hazards following a recent disaster that killed more than 1,400 people, officials said.

Search crews continue to look for more than 5,000 missing people in the border region near China after an August glacier collapse triggered devastating landslides and flash floods through local valleys.

"Our aim is to lower the risk of the lakes breaching their natural dams and reduce the risk of downstream floods," said Subash Tuladhar, an expert with a government research centre tasked with overseeing the new project.

