Regulated canals will divert water into adjacent rivers, Tuladhar said, noting that crews face health risks from prolonged work at high elevations alongside the danger of sudden flooding.

Preliminary design work and detailed studies targeting the central and eastern lakes of Thulagi, Lower Barun, Lumding Tsho, and Hongu II will begin during the fiscal year ending in mid-July 2027, according to authorities.

open image in gallery {"id":"trigger-autogallery-58577","index":0} Mud covered properties following a flash flood at Trishuli in Nuwakot district, Nepal, August 26, 2026 ( REUTERS )

Perched at altitudes ranging between 4,050 meters and 5,483 meters (13,280 feet to 18,000 feet), the water bodies are among the most dangerous in the country, according to a 2020 report by the United Nations Development Programme and the International Centre for Integrated Mountain Development (ICIMOD).

if (document.cookie.split(';').some(cookie => cookie.trim() === '__DEBUG__=true')) { console.log('Ad logs: "taboola-carousel-thumbnails", injectedAtParagraph: 6'); }

Tuladhar, of Nepal's Centre for Hydrology and Water Resources Research, stated that the country has the technical capability to undertake the $50-million initiative, which will receive funding from the Green Climate Fund, the UNDP, and the domestic government.

DRAINING INITIATIVE MAY TAKE SIX YEARS

The drainage work using gated structures will begin after detailed assessments and could require up to six years to complete, said Sanjeeb Baral, the Centre's executive director.

