Receding glaciers, unstable mountain terrain, and unpredictable river flows demonstrate the high price the nation is paying, he added.

Nepal has recorded roughly 26 Glacial Lake Outburst Flood events over the past four decades, losing an average of more than 300 lives and millions of dollars in property to extreme climate incidents every year.

The latest flooding damaged 12 hydroelectric stations, alongside roads, bridges, agricultural lands, and residential areas. Officials noted that cash-strapped Nepal will require nearly $5 billion for initial recovery efforts.

open image in gallery {"id":"trigger-autogallery-58578","index":1} Search crews continue to look for more than 5,000 missing people after an August glacier collapse triggered devastating landslides and flash floods through local valleys ( REUTERS )

Climate scientists note that rising global temperatures cause Himalayan glaciers to recede, creating precarious lakes impounded by little more than loose rock and ice above valleys where millions live.

Reducing lake levels by 3 meters (10 feet) would substantially lower the risk, said Samjwal Ratna Bajracharya, a retired ICIMOD expert on glaciers, glacial lakes, and outburst floods.

"They are growing fast, are high up in the Himalayas and could cause outbursts any time, due to earthquakes, avalanches, climatic conditions and unplanned human-induced activity," he told Reuters.
